<?php

class Queue {

    private $head;
    private $tail;
    private $size;

    public function __construct() {
        $this->head = null;
        $this->tail = null;
        $this->size = 0;
    }

    /**
     * Adds an item to the end of the queue.
     * @param $data The data to be added to the queue.
     */
    public function enqueue($data) {
        $newListItem = new QueueItem($data);

        $newListItem->prev = $this->tail;

        if ($this->tail !== null) {
            $this->tail->next = $newListItem;
        } else {
            // If queue is empty, set head to new item
            $this->head = $newListItem;
        }

        $this->tail = $newListItem;
        $this->size++;
    }

    /**
     * Removes and returns the item at the front of the queue.
     * @return mixed The data that was removed from the queue.
     * @throws Exception If the queue is empty.
     */
    public function dequeue() {
        if ($this->isEmpty()) {
            throw new Exception("Queue is empty");
        }

        $data = $this->head->data;
        $this->head = $this->head->next;

        if ($this->head === null) {
            $this->tail = null;
        } else {
            $this->head->prev = null;
        }

        $this->size--;

        return $data;
    }

    /**
     * Returns the item at the front of the queue without removing it.
     * @return mixed The data at the front of the queue.
     * @throws Exception If the queue is empty.
     */
    public function peek() {
        if ($this->isEmpty()) {
            throw new Exception("Queue is empty");
        }

        return $this->head->data;
    }

    /**
     * Returns true if the queue is empty and false if it contains one or more items.
     * @return bool True if the queue is empty, false otherwise.
     */
    public function isEmpty() {
        return $this->size === 0;
    }

}

class QueueItem {

    public $data;
    public $prev;
    public $next;

    public function __construct($data) {
        $this->data = $data;
        $this->prev = null;
        $this->next = null;
    }

}
```

This implementation of a queue data structure uses a doubly linked list to ensure efficient enqueueing and dequeueing operations. The `Queue` class has methods for enqueueing, dequeueing, peeking, and checking if the queue is empty. 

The `enqueue()` method creates a new `QueueItem` object and sets its `prev` property to the current tail of the queue. If the queue is not empty, the `next` property of the current tail is set to the new item, otherwise the `head` property is set to the new item. The `tail` is then updated to the new item, and the `size` of the queue is incremented.

The `dequeue()` method removes and returns the item at the front of the queue. If the queue is empty, an exception is thrown. The `head` property is set to the next item in the queue, and if the new `head` is `null` (meaning the queue is now empty), the `tail` property is set to `null` as well. The `size` of the queue is then decremented.

The `peek()` method returns the data at the front of the queue without removing it. If the queue is empty, an exception is thrown.

The `isEmpty()` method returns `true` if the `size` of the queue is 0, and `false` otherwise.

The `QueueItem` class is a simple class that holds an item of data and pointers to the previous and next items in the queue.

